"""Adult course, units 13 and 14: the Mass line by line, and death and what follows.
These revisit ideas from the younger grades at the level of a thinking adult."""
from spec import L, U

A = [
U('The Mass, line by line', 'Sacraments', 'What is actually happening at Mass, and how do I take part?', [
 L('The Liturgy of the Word', 'The first half of Mass is not a warm-up. In it Christ speaks to his people, and the Church answers with a psalm, a creed and prayers. The readings are laid out so that over three years most of the Bible is heard.', '1100,1101,1102,1103,1346,1349,1154,1155', 'Luke 24:13-35', 'liturgy scripture homily creed', 'Why does Mass begin with so much reading and listening?', 'Take this Sunday’s readings from the Readings page. Before Mass, read the Gospel twice and write down one phrase to listen for.', '',
   teach=['The Mass has two parts that form one act of worship (CCC 1346). In the Liturgy of the Word the Church is fed by Scripture; in the Liturgy of the Eucharist she is fed by the Body of Christ. Luke 24 shows the same shape: Jesus opens the Scriptures on the road, and then is known in the breaking of the bread.', 'On Sundays there are normally three readings and a psalm: an Old Testament reading, a New Testament letter, and the Gospel, which is heard standing. The Old and New Testaments are chosen to speak to each other, and the Sunday cycle (A, B, C) takes the Church through Matthew, Mark and Luke, with John in the seasons of Lent and Easter. Weekday Mass has a two-year cycle of first readings.', 'The homily is part of the liturgy, not an interruption (CCC 1154). The Creed that follows is the people’s answer: we say what we have heard and believe. The prayers of the faithful end the first half with the Church’s own priesthood at work, praying for the world.'],
   talk=['When have you heard a reading at Mass as if for the first time?', 'What helps you listen, and what gets in the way?', 'Why might the Church have people stand for the Gospel?'],
   misc=['“The readings are just a warm-up for the real part.”', '“The homily is the priest’s opinion.”'],
   quiz=['What are the two main parts of the Mass?|Entrance and dismissal|Liturgy of the Word and Liturgy of the Eucharist|Confession and Communion|Music and silence|1|CCC 1346.', 'Over how many years does the Sunday cycle take the Church through the Gospels?|One|Two|Three|Ten|2|Years A, B and C.', 'Why do we say the Creed after the homily?|To fill time|It is our answer to the Word we have heard|Only to teach children|It is optional|1|We profess what we have received.'],
   life='Read the Sunday Gospel on Saturday night, and listen on Sunday for what you did not notice when reading.', saint='Justin Martyr: around the year 155 he described the Sunday gathering in Rome, with readings, a homily, prayers and the Eucharist, so clearly that we recognize it today.',
   deeper=[], mem='“Were not our hearts burning within us while he spoke to us on the road?” (Luke 24:32)', hook='Ask: “If a stranger visited your parish, what would they say the first half of Mass is for?” Write three answers.', assess='Ask each person to name the parts of the Liturgy of the Word in order and say what each is for.',
   tryit=['Find this Sunday’s readings and read the Gospel twice.', 'Write down the Old Testament reading’s link to the Gospel.', 'Listen at Mass for one phrase you wrote down.'],
   saintStory='Justin was born around the year 100 in Flavia Neapolis (modern Nablus) to pagan parents. He went from philosopher to philosopher, a Stoic, a Peripatetic, a Pythagorean and a Platonist, looking for the truth about God. Walking by the sea one day, an old man pointed him to the Hebrew prophets, and then to Christ. “Immediately a flame was kindled in my soul,” he later wrote. He kept his philosopher’s cloak and opened a school in Rome.\nAround the year 155 he wrote an Apology to the emperor Antoninus Pius, explaining what Christians do. He describes a Sunday gathering: the memoirs of the apostles or the writings of the prophets are read as long as time permits; the president speaks and urges the people to imitate these good things; they stand and pray; bread and wine mixed with water are brought, thanks are offered at length, and the people answer “Amen.” The deacons carry the Eucharist to those who are absent. Read it and you can follow your own parish Mass.\nHe was denounced by a rival philosopher and tried before the prefect Rusticus around 165. Asked whether he believed he would go to heaven if he was beheaded, he answered that he did not suppose it, but knew and was fully persuaded of it. He and his companions refused to sacrifice to the gods and were beheaded. The Church keeps his memorial on June 1.',
   script=dict(open='Pass out a blank card to each person. Ask, “Finish this sentence honestly: the first half of Mass is…” Collect and read a few aloud without names. Say, “Tonight we look at why the Church puts Scripture at the start of her highest prayer.”',
     teach=['Say: “The Mass is one act of worship in two tables: the table of the Word and the table of the Eucharist (CCC 1346–1347). Vatican II said the Church has always venerated the Scriptures as she venerates the Body of the Lord, since from both she takes the bread of life. Read Luke 24:13–35 slowly together. Notice what Jesus does first: he does not reveal himself; he opens the Scriptures. Only then is he known in the breaking of the bread. The Mass follows the pattern of Emmaus.”', 'Say: “On a Sunday: a reading from the Old Testament; a responsorial psalm, which is itself Scripture, sung or said by the people; a reading from a New Testament letter; the Alleluia, or in Lent another acclamation, as we stand to greet the Lord who is about to speak in the Gospel; the Gospel; the homily; the Creed on Sundays and solemnities; and the prayers of the faithful. Weekdays have two readings. The three-year Sunday cycle was introduced after Vatican II so that, over time, the faithful would hear most of Scripture.”', 'Say: “What are we doing as we listen? The Catechism says that in the liturgy God speaks to his people and Christ is present in his Word (CCC 1088, 1100–1102). Listening is therefore not passive; it is the first act of participation. Practical help: read the Gospel beforehand; pick one phrase and carry it; do not treat the homily as a performance to grade. The Creed is our response: we say Amen to what we have heard. And the prayers of the faithful are where the baptized exercise their priesthood by praying for the Church, the world, the suffering and the dead.”'],
     ask=['What are the parts of the Liturgy of the Word, in order? (Readings and psalm, Gospel, homily, Creed, prayers of the faithful.)', 'What pattern from Luke 24 does the Mass follow? (Jesus opens the Scriptures, then is known in the breaking of the bread.)', 'Why is listening part of participation? (Christ is present in his Word, and we answer with faith.)'],
     practice=['Hand out the coming Sunday’s readings. Read the Gospel aloud twice, with a minute of silence between.', 'In pairs, each person says the one phrase they will listen for.', 'Together write three intentions as if for the prayers of the faithful, one for the Church, one for the world and one for someone who suffers.'],
     close='Pray the Our Father together. Remind the group to read the Gospel before Sunday and bring one phrase back to share next time.')),
 L('The Eucharistic Prayer', 'At the center of Mass the priest, in the person of Christ, offers the prayer in which bread and wine become the Body and Blood of Christ. The Church calls this the memorial of Christ’s sacrifice, and what happens is real, not a symbol only.', '1352,1353,1354,1362,1363,1364,1365,1366,1367,1373,1374,1375,1376', '1 Corinthians 11:23-26', 'eucharist real-presence transubstantiation sacrifice', 'What does it mean that Mass is a sacrifice, and that the bread is really Christ?', 'Read the words of consecration in 1 Corinthians 11 and Luke 22. Write what is the same and what the Church adds when she prays them.', '',
   teach=['Every Eucharistic Prayer has the same parts (CCC 1352–1354): thanksgiving (the Preface), the acclamation (Holy, Holy, Holy), the epiclesis (calling on the Spirit to make the gifts holy), the account of the institution and consecration, the memorial, the offering, the intercessions for the Church and the dead, and the final doxology, to which the people answer “Amen.”', 'The Church teaches that the Mass is the same sacrifice as the Cross: not a new one, and not a repetition, but the one sacrifice made present (CCC 1364–1367). “Memorial” in the biblical sense does not mean only remembering; it means that what God did once becomes present and active for us now, as Israel at Passover does not only remember the Exodus but takes part in it.', 'After the consecration, Christ is truly, really and substantially present: Body, Blood, soul and divinity (CCC 1374). The Church calls the change transubstantiation (CCC 1376). It does not claim to explain how, only that what looks like bread and wine is, in reality, Christ. That is why we kneel or bow, and why the Church keeps the Eucharist in the tabernacle.'],
   talk=['What do you notice about the words of institution, and why do you think they are said so slowly?', 'How does the idea of memorial in Passover help you understand the Mass?', 'What changes in how you act at Mass if you believe this?'],
   misc=['“The Mass is a re-crucifixion.”', '“The Eucharist is only a symbol.”'],
   quiz=['What is the epiclesis?|A closing hymn|The prayer calling on the Holy Spirit to make the gifts holy|A reading from Acts|The Our Father|1|CCC 1353.', 'The Mass is…|A new sacrifice every time|The one sacrifice of Christ made present|Only a meal|Only a symbol|1|CCC 1366.', 'What does “transubstantiation” name?|The change of the whole substance of bread and wine into the Body and Blood of Christ|A kind of music|A feast day|A vestment|0|CCC 1376.'],
   life='At the next Mass, follow the Eucharistic Prayer with your eyes closed and listen for the epiclesis, the institution, and the Amen.', saint='Thomas Aquinas: the great teacher of the real presence, who also wrote the hymns for Corpus Christi, including Pange Lingua and Tantum Ergo.',
   deeper=['room:aug-confessions'], mem='“This is my body, which is for you. Do this in remembrance of me.” (1 Corinthians 11:24)', hook='Ask: “If someone told you the Eucharist is a symbol, what would you say, and what would you want them to know about Passover?”', assess='Ask each person to name three parts of the Eucharistic Prayer and say what the Church means by the Mass being a sacrifice.',
   tryit=['Read 1 Corinthians 11:23–26 and Luke 22:14–20 side by side.', 'Underline the parts of the Eucharistic Prayer that echo them.', 'Write one sentence on what “memorial” means here.'],
   saintStory='Thomas was born around 1225 near Aquino, Italy, the youngest son of a noble family. At about five he was given to the Benedictines at Monte Cassino. Later, as a student in Naples, he joined the new Dominican order. His family was furious: his brothers seized him on the road and held him for about a year in the family castle. They even sent a woman to tempt him; he drove her out with a burning stick from the fire. In time his mother relented and he escaped.\nHe studied in Paris and Cologne under Albert the Great, and was called “the dumb ox” by classmates because he was large and quiet. Albert said that the ox would one day bellow so loudly it would fill the world. He taught in Paris, Italy and Naples, and wrote the Summa Theologiae, an immense work on God, humanity and Christ. At the request of Pope Urban IV he composed the Office and Mass for the feast of Corpus Christi, with the hymns Pange Lingua, Lauda Sion and Adoro Te Devote, still sung today.\nOn December 6, 1273, after Mass, he stopped writing. Asked why, he said that all he had written seemed like straw compared with what had been revealed to him. He died on March 7, 1274, on his way to the Council of Lyons, at about forty-nine. He was canonized in 1323 and named a Doctor of the Church in 1567.',
   script=dict(open='Start with silence. Ask the group to look at an object on the table, a loaf of bread, and say what it is. Then ask, “What would it mean if I told you that at Mass, what looks like this is not bread anymore?” Let the question sit before you speak.',
     teach=['Say: “The Eucharistic Prayer is the center of the Mass. Trace the parts with me: thanksgiving, the Holy, Holy, Holy, the epiclesis, the account of the institution, the memorial, the offering, the intercessions and the final doxology (CCC 1352–1355). Notice the structure is the old Jewish blessing prayer, raised: first we thank, then we ask, then we bless. In the Roman rite there are several Eucharistic Prayers, and the priest says one of them. Each has these same parts.”', 'Say: “In the Bible, to remember is to make present. At Passover, Jewish families say, ‘We were slaves in Egypt’; the generations of the Exodus are made present to them. In the same way the Church does what Jesus commanded, ‘Do this in remembrance of me,’ and the one sacrifice of the Cross is made present on the altar (CCC 1362–1367). It is the same priest, Christ, and the same victim; only the manner is different, without blood, sacramentally. Not a new death, not a mere recalling.”', 'Say: “The Church teaches that Christ is present in the Eucharist in a unique way: truly, really and substantially, with his Body and Blood, soul and divinity (CCC 1374). After the consecration the appearances of bread and wine remain, but their substance is Christ’s. The Council of Trent called this change transubstantiation. The Church is not claiming to explain the mechanism; she is saying what is there. John 6 shows the people in Capernaum struggling with the same thing, and many left. Jesus did not soften it. This is why Catholics genuflect, keep silence, and fast before Communion.”'],
     ask=['What are the parts of the Eucharistic Prayer? (Preface, Sanctus, epiclesis, institution, memorial, offering, intercessions, doxology.)', 'What does it mean that the Mass is a memorial? (The one sacrifice of Christ is made present.)', 'What does the Church mean by transubstantiation? (The substance changes; the appearances remain.)'],
     practice=['Read 1 Corinthians 11:23–26 together and underline the phrase ‘do this’.', 'In pairs, explain to each other, in plain language, why the Mass is not a new sacrifice.', 'Each person writes a one-sentence reply to a friend who says, ‘Isn’t it just a symbol?’ and shares one.'],
     close='Pray Aquinas’s hymn Tantum Ergo, or the Act of Faith, together. Invite everyone to kneel or bow at the next consecration with fresh attention.')),
 L('Communion and being sent', 'The Mass ends with Communion and a sending. Receiving the Eucharist unites us to Christ and to each other, and then we are sent out to live what we have received.', '1331,1332,1385,1386,1387,1388,1389,1391,1392,1393,1394,1395,1396,1397,1398,1399,1400', 'John 6:51-58', 'eucharist body-of-christ mass', 'How do I prepare for Communion, and what is the Mass sending me to do?', 'Write a simple routine for before and after Communion: what you do and say. Keep it short enough to do every Sunday.', '',
   teach=['The Church asks Catholics to receive Communion in the state of grace, which means with no unconfessed mortal sin (CCC 1385, 1415), after fasting for one hour from food and drink (water and medicine excepted), and with reverence. Catholics are bound to receive at least once a year during the Easter season (CCC 2042). The Church invites frequent reception, even daily.', 'The Eucharist produces effects (CCC 1391–1397): it unites us to Christ, separates us from sin, strengthens charity, commits us to the poor, and makes the Church one (CCC 1396). Paul’s warning in 1 Corinthians 11:27–29 and his teaching that we who are many are one body because we share one loaf (1 Corinthians 10:17) belong together.', 'Ite, missa est: the Latin dismissal gives the Mass its name (CCC 1332). “Go” is not a way of ending; it is the start of the mission. The Catechism says the Eucharist commits us to the poor (CCC 1397). What you received, you are to become: the Body of Christ in the world.'],
   talk=['What do you do before and after receiving Communion, and why?', 'Why does the Church link Communion with caring for the poor?', 'What would it look like to live the dismissal this week?'],
   misc=['“Communion is a private moment between me and God.”', '“It is okay to receive in any state.”'],
   quiz=['How long is the usual fast before Communion?|None|One hour from food and drink|Twelve hours|All day|1|Water and medicine are excepted.', 'What do the Catechism and Paul link to the Eucharist?|Wealth|Unity of the Church and care for the poor|Politics|Study|1|CCC 1396–1397.', 'What does “Ite, missa est” mean at the end of Mass?|The Mass is over for good|Go, you are sent|Please sit|Pray silently|1|The word “Mass” comes from the dismissal.'],
   life='Stay for two minutes of thanksgiving after Communion next Sunday, then decide on one concrete way to carry it out the door.', saint='Pius X: the pope who invited the faithful to frequent Communion and lowered the age for first Communion so that children could receive.',
   deeper=['apol:confession'], mem='“Because there is one loaf, we who are many are one body, for we all partake of the one loaf.” (1 Corinthians 10:17)', hook='Ask: “What are you thinking about in the minute after you receive Communion? What would you like to be thinking about?”', assess='Ask each person to state the conditions for receiving Communion and the Catechism’s three effects of the Eucharist.',
   tryit=['Write a three-line thanksgiving to say after Communion.', 'Plan one concrete act of service to follow this Sunday.', 'Ask someone at your parish about the ministry that brings Communion to the sick.'],
   saintStory='Giuseppe Sarto was born in 1835 in Riese, a village in the Veneto, to a poor family; his father was a municipal messenger and his mother a seamstress. He walked miles to school, and later to the seminary, sometimes carrying his shoes to save them. He was ordained in 1858 and spent almost twenty years as a parish priest, then became bishop of Mantua, patriarch of Venice, and in 1903 pope, taking the name Pius X. He is said to have been reluctant, even asking the cardinals to choose someone else.\nHe made the Eucharist his great project. In 1905 the decree Sacra Tridentina invited Catholics to frequent, even daily, Communion, and told confessors not to hold back those who were in the state of grace and had a right intention. In 1910 the decree Quam Singulari lowered the age for first Communion to the age of reason, about seven, so that children could receive. In the same years he reformed church music, the breviary and the catechism, and condemned the movement called Modernism.\nHe died on August 20, 1914, as the First World War broke out, saying the war would break his heart. He was canonized in 1954. His own priests said he always remained a village priest who knew everyone’s name. His body is in St Peter’s Basilica.',
   script=dict(open='Ask: “What do you do in the five minutes before Mass starts? Be honest.” Write responses on a board and keep them in view. Say, “Tonight we ask what the Church hopes we do before, during and after Communion, and why the Mass ends with an order.”',
     teach=['Say: “First, what are we receiving? John 6:51–58: ‘my flesh is true food and my blood is true drink.’ The Church takes it literally (CCC 1336–1338). Then, how do we receive? The Church asks for the state of grace, which means that anyone conscious of grave sin should go to Confession first (CCC 1385, 1415); a fast of one hour from food and drink; reverence of body and heart; and an annual minimum of Communion in the Easter season (CCC 2042). Non-Catholics are warmly invited to pray for a blessing but not to receive Communion, because Communion signifies full unity of faith, sacraments and communion with the Church (CCC 1398–1401).”', 'Say: “What does Communion do? The Catechism lists effects (CCC 1391–1397). It increases our union with Christ and with the Church; it preserves, increases and renews the life of grace; it separates us from sin and strengthens us against future sins; it forgives venial sins; it commits us to the poor; and it makes the Church. Paul’s image in 1 Corinthians 10:17 is the loaf: many grains, one bread. It is impossible to receive the Body of Christ and ignore its members. This is why Communion processions are sometimes accompanied by collections for the poor, a practice as old as Justin Martyr.”', 'Say: “Last, the dismissal. The word ‘Mass’ comes from Ite, missa est, ‘Go, it is sent’ (CCC 1332). The liturgy ends with a mission, and the Church offers several forms of the dismissal. After Communion, give thanks; the Church recommends a moment of silence. Many saints taught a short prayer of thanksgiving. Then go: the point of the Mass is not only the hour, but the week that follows. Ask: where did you meet Christ on Sunday, and who will meet him in you on Monday?”'],
     ask=['What does the Church ask before receiving Communion? (State of grace, one-hour fast, reverence.)', 'Name two effects of Communion from the Catechism. (Union with Christ, forgiveness of venial sins, commitment to the poor, unity of the Church.)', 'What does the dismissal mean? (We are sent to live what we have received.)'],
     practice=['Write a three-line prayer to say after Communion and read it to a partner.', 'List three concrete ways to carry Sunday into Monday.', 'Role-play a short, kind reply to a friend who asks why non-Catholics cannot receive.'],
     close='Pray the Anima Christi aloud together, or a short thanksgiving. Invite each person to choose one act of service before next Sunday.')),
 ]),
U('Death, judgment and hope', 'Creed', 'What does the Church teach about sickness, death and what comes after?', [
 L('Sickness and the Anointing of the Sick', 'Illness is a human question and a spiritual one. In the Anointing of the Sick the Church prays over the ill, anoints them with oil, and asks Christ to heal and strengthen them. It is for the living, not only the dying.', '1499,1500,1501,1502,1503,1504,1505,1506,1510,1511,1512,1513,1514,1515,1516,1517,1520,1521,1522,1523,1524,1525', 'James 5:13-15', 'anointing sacrament priest grace', 'What is the Anointing of the Sick for, and when should someone ask for it?', 'Find out how your parish arranges Anointing and visits to the sick. Write the phone number of the parish office where you will remember it.', '',
   teach=['Jesus healed the sick, sent his disciples to heal, and identified with the sick (Matthew 25:36). James 5:14–15 is the Church’s basis for the sacrament: “Is any among you sick? Let him call for the elders of the church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord.” The sacrament is given only by priests and bishops (CCC 1516).', 'It is for any Catholic who has reached the age of reason and is in danger from serious illness, old age, or before a serious operation (CCC 1514–1515). It can be repeated if the illness worsens or the person recovers and falls ill again. It is not only for the dying: the Church calls the sacrament given at the point of death by another name, Viaticum, which is Holy Communion for the journey (CCC 1524).', 'The effects (CCC 1520–1523): the grace of strength, peace and courage to face illness; union with the Passion of Christ; forgiveness of sins if the person cannot confess; sometimes physical healing, if it serves salvation; and preparation for the passage to eternal life. The Church does not promise healing of the body, but does promise the presence of Christ.'],
   talk=['What fears come up when you think of illness or old age?', 'Why does the Church make the Anointing a sacrament for the living?', 'Who in your life should be offered a visit or the sacrament?'],
   misc=['“Last rites means the person is about to die.”', '“If the Anointing works, the person will be cured.”'],
   quiz=['Who may administer the Anointing of the Sick?|Any layperson|Only priests and bishops|Only the Pope|Doctors|1|CCC 1516.', 'Anointing may be received…|Only by the dying|By those in serious illness, before major surgery, or in old age|Only once in a lifetime|By unbaptized people|1|CCC 1514–1515.', 'What is Viaticum?|A hymn|Holy Communion for the journey, given to the dying|Last confession|Blessed oil|1|CCC 1524.'],
   life='Write down the name of someone who is ill or frail. Visit or call this week, and offer to ask the parish for a visit from the priest.', saint='Camillus de Lellis: a soldier and gambler who became a priest and founded an order of men who cared for the sick; their red cross on black cassocks inspired later hospital symbols.',
   deeper=[], mem='“The prayer of faith will save the sick man, and the Lord will raise him up.” (James 5:15)', hook='Ask: “If you or someone you love were seriously ill tomorrow, what would you want from the Church? What would you be afraid to ask?”', assess='Ask each person to say who may receive the Anointing, who gives it, and what it does.',
   tryit=['Find out how to request Anointing at your parish.', 'Write the name of someone to visit, and when.', 'Read the Rite’s prayer over the oil, if you can find it.'],
   saintStory='Camillus was born in 1550 in Bucchianico, Italy, a very tall young man with a violent temper and a gambling habit. He became a soldier like his father, fighting for Venice and later Naples, and gambled away almost everything, even his shirt. A sore on his leg that never healed sent him for treatment to the San Giacomo hospital in Rome, where he worked as a servant to pay his way, but was dismissed for gambling again.\nIn 1575 he heard a sermon at a Capuchin friary and was converted. He tried to join the Capuchins, but his leg wound kept reopening and they could not take him. He went back to San Giacomo and served the sick, and in time became its administrator. He saw how patients were neglected, and the dying left without a priest. With the help of his confessor, Philip Neri, he was ordained a priest in 1584 and founded the Ministers of the Sick, who took a fourth vow to serve the sick even at the risk of their lives. They nursed plague victims and soldiers in the field.\nHe taught that a nurse should treat each patient as if he were Christ himself. He died on July 14, 1614, in Rome, after years of illness of his own. He was canonized in 1746 and named patron of the sick, hospitals and nurses.',
   script=dict(open='Begin by asking everyone to think silently of a time they were in a hospital or visited someone in one. Ask for one word that describes how it felt, and write the words on the board. Say, “Tonight we ask what Christ offers in those moments, through the Church.”',
     teach=['Say: “Jesus spent a large part of his public life healing the sick. He told the disciples to do likewise (Mark 6:13), and the Church continues this ministry. The Anointing of the Sick is rooted in James 5:14–15 and was practiced by the early Church. For centuries it was delayed until death, so that people called it ‘extreme unction’ and feared the priest’s arrival. Vatican II restored its meaning: it is for those who begin to be in danger of death through sickness or old age (CCC 1514).”', 'Say: “What happens in the rite? The priest lays hands on the person in silence, and anoints the forehead and hands with the oil of the sick, blessed by the bishop at Holy Week, with the words: ‘Through this holy anointing may the Lord in his love and mercy help you with the grace of the Holy Spirit. May the Lord who frees you from sin save you and raise you up.’ The sacrament may be given in the home, hospital or church, and often together with Confession and Communion. If a person is dying, the priest gives Viaticum, Communion for the passage (CCC 1524–1525).”', 'Say: “What are the effects? Strengthening against discouragement and temptation, especially at the hour of death; union with the Passion of Christ for the good of the Church; the forgiveness of sins; and sometimes healing of the body, if it is conducive to the salvation of the soul (CCC 1520–1523). The Church never promises a cure. She promises that the sick are not alone. Practical points: call the priest early; ask him to come before surgery; do not wait until someone is unconscious; family members should be present if possible.”'],
     ask=['Who may give the Anointing of the Sick? (A priest or bishop.)', 'Who may receive it? (A baptized Catholic with serious illness, before surgery or in old age.)', 'What are its effects? (Strength, peace, union with Christ’s Passion, forgiveness of sins, sometimes healing.)'],
     practice=['Role-play a short call to the parish office asking for a priest to visit a sick relative.', 'List what a good visit to a sick person includes, and what it avoids.', 'Write a short prayer for someone who is ill and read it to a partner.'],
     close='Pray the Hail Mary together for the sick, using the names the group has offered. Ask each to find the parish’s sick-call number before the next meeting.')),
 L('Death, judgment, heaven and hell', 'Catholics believe that death is not the end, that each person is judged at death and again at the end of the world, and that we are made for heaven. Hell is real and freely chosen, but God wants everyone to be saved.', '1020,1021,1022,1023,1024,1025,1026,1027,1028,1029,1033,1034,1035,1036,1037,1038,1039,1040,1041,1058', 'Matthew 25:31-46', 'judgment heaven hell eternal-life eschatology', 'What happens when we die, and what does the Church mean by heaven and hell?', 'Read Matthew 25:31–46 and list what the people are judged on. Pick one item and plan a concrete act this week.', '',
   teach=['The Church teaches that at death the soul is separated from the body, and each person faces a particular judgment (CCC 1021–1022). The choices of this life matter; there is no reincarnation (CCC 1013). At the end of time Christ will return and the dead will rise, and the general judgment will reveal everything (CCC 1038–1041).', 'Heaven is life with the Trinity, the Blessed Virgin, the angels and all the blessed (CCC 1023–1026). It is not a cloud with harps, but the vision of God face to face, a perfect fulfilment of what our heart desires (CCC 1028). Those who die in friendship with God but who need purification go through purgatory first.', 'Hell is the state of definitive self-exclusion from communion with God (CCC 1033). The Church teaches that it exists, and that those who die in mortal sin without repentance go there (CCC 1035), but she has never declared any individual person to be in hell. God wants all to be saved (1 Timothy 2:4). The Church asks us to take this seriously, as a warning and a measure of the dignity of our freedom.'],
   talk=['What makes it difficult to believe in hell? What does the Church say in reply?', 'How does Matthew 25 change your view of what counts at judgment?', 'How does the hope of heaven shape how you live now?'],
   misc=['“Everyone goes to heaven.”', '“The Church says certain people are in hell.”'],
   quiz=['What is the particular judgment?|The judgment of each person at death|The last day|A court|A feast|0|CCC 1021–1022.', 'What does the Church teach about naming individuals in hell?|She names the damned|She has never declared any individual to be in hell|Only popes can be there|Nothing at all|1|The Church canonizes saints but never names the damned.', 'What is heaven, in essence?|A place of rest|The vision of God and life with the Trinity, Mary and the saints|Forgetting the world|A reward only for monks|1|CCC 1023–1028.'],
   life='Read Matthew 25 once a day this week. Each day, ask: where did I meet Christ, and did I recognize him?', saint='Thomas More: he faced death with faith and humor, and wrote in the Tower of London about the last things with calm.',
   deeper=['apol:hell'], mem='“Come, you who are blessed by my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world.” (Matthew 25:34)', hook='Ask: “If you could ask God one question about the afterlife, what would it be?” Write questions and return to them at the end.', assess='Ask each person to explain the difference between the particular and the general judgment and say what the Church teaches about hell.',
   tryit=['Write your answer to “What is heaven?” in two sentences.', 'List the acts Matthew 25 names and mark which you did this month.', 'Choose one act of mercy to do this week.'],
   saintStory='Thomas More was born in London in 1478, the son of a judge, and trained as a lawyer. He married Jane Colt and had four children; after her death he married Alice Middleton. He wrote Utopia in 1516 and rose from a lawyer to the Lord Chancellor of England under Henry VIII in 1529. At home he educated his daughters like his sons, unusual for his time, and kept a lively household with pets and a fool.\nThe king wanted his marriage to Catherine of Aragon annulled so that he could marry Anne Boleyn, and More was unwilling to accept the king as head of the Church in England. He resigned as Chancellor in 1532, lived modestly, and kept silent. In 1534 he refused to swear the Oath of Succession because it required him to repudiate the pope’s authority. He was imprisoned in the Tower of London for fifteen months. His daughter Margaret visited and begged him to give in; he would not violate his conscience.\nIn prison he wrote on the Passion and on comfort in tribulation. He was tried for treason in 1535 and found guilty on the testimony of Richard Rich, a man who later admitted to lying. On July 6, 1535, he was beheaded on Tower Hill. He is said to have told the executioner to be of good cheer, that his neck was very short. He was canonized in 1935 and is patron of lawyers and politicians.',
   script=dict(open='Hand each person two index cards. Say, “On one write what you hope heaven is. On the other, what you fear hell is. No names.” Collect, shuffle and read a few aloud. Say, “Tonight we compare our picture with what the Church teaches.”',
     teach=['Say: “At death the soul is separated from the body. The particular judgment (CCC 1021–1022): at once the soul receives its eternal retribution, whether through purification, immediate entrance into heaven, or immediate and everlasting damnation (CCC 1022). This is not a panic. It is the Church’s way of saying that our choices matter and our freedom is real. The Church rejects reincarnation (CCC 1013): we die once. At the end, Christ will return, the dead will be raised, and the general judgment will make plain the meaning of history (CCC 1038–1041).”', 'Say: “Heaven. Notice what the Catechism says (CCC 1023–1029). It is the ultimate end and fulfilment of the deepest human longings, the state of supreme and definitive happiness. It is to be with Christ. It is to see God ‘as he is’ (1 John 3:2). Scripture uses images: a wedding feast, a city, a garden, rest. Mary, the angels and saints are there. The resurrection of the body means heaven is not only a spiritual escape; it is a new heaven and a new earth (Revelation 21:1).”', 'Say: “Hell is the hardest teaching, and the Church does not hide it. CCC 1033–1037: to die in mortal sin without repenting and accepting God’s merciful love means remaining separated from him forever by our own free choice. God does not predestine anyone to hell (CCC 1037). The Church prays that all be saved. She has never said any person is in hell; she says Judas’s end is not declared, and she canonizes saints, but does not canonize the lost. C. S. Lewis called the doors of hell locked from the inside. Our response: take our freedom seriously, trust God’s mercy, and do what Matthew 25 says.”'],
     ask=['What happens at the particular judgment? (Each person’s eternal destiny is decided, through heaven, purification or hell.)', 'What does the Church teach about naming individuals in hell? (Never declared any person to be there.)', 'What is heaven in essence? (Seeing God face to face and life with the saints, in a new creation.)'],
     practice=['In pairs, read Matthew 25:31–46 and list the six works of mercy it names.', 'Write a two-sentence reply to someone who says, “A loving God wouldn’t allow hell.”', 'Plan one specific work of mercy for this week and tell the group.'],
     close='Pray the prayer from the Rite of Funerals, “Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord,” together. Ask each person to read Matthew 25 every day this week.')),
 L('Purgatory, praying for the dead and the funeral', 'Catholics pray for the dead because most of us die needing to be made ready to meet God. Purgatory is the Church’s name for that purification. The funeral rites, the Mass for the dead and the cemetery are a work of mercy and hope.', '1030,1031,1032,1054,1055,1371,1680,1681,1682,1683,1684,1685,1686,1687,1688,1689,1690', '2 Maccabees 12:38-46', 'purgatory intercession communion-of-saints mass works-of-mercy', 'Why do we pray for the dead, and what does the Church do when someone dies?', 'Make a list of the people you want to remember in prayer. Add their names to your calendar on their anniversary or on All Souls’ Day, November 2.', '',
   teach=['Purgatory is the final purification of those who die in God’s friendship but are not yet fully purified (CCC 1030–1031). It is not a second chance or a lesser hell. Those in purgatory are saved. The Church’s doctrine rests on the ancient practice of prayer for the dead (2 Maccabees 12:38–46), on 1 Corinthians 3:10–15, and on the belief that nothing unclean can enter heaven (Revelation 21:27).', 'The Church urges prayer for the dead, almsgiving, indulgences and works of penance for them, and above all the offering of Mass (CCC 1032, 1371). The communion of saints is not broken by death. Those on earth pray for those who have died, the saints pray for us, and the souls in purgatory can intercede for us too. All Souls’ Day, November 2, is set aside for this.', 'The Catholic funeral has three stages (CCC 1684–1690): the vigil, with prayer and remembrance; the funeral liturgy, which is normally a Mass; and the committal at the grave. The Church prefers burial and permits cremation where it is not chosen in denial of the resurrection, but asks that the ashes be kept in a sacred place and not scattered or kept at home (Ad resurgendum cum Christo, 2016). The funeral is a time of mourning and hope, not of canonizing the dead.'],
   talk=['What do you think prayers for the dead do?', 'Who in your life do you wish you had prayed for more?', 'What would you want your funeral to say about your faith?'],
   misc=['“Purgatory is a second chance after death.”', '“A funeral Mass is a celebration of the person’s life only.”'],
   quiz=['What is purgatory?|A second chance|The final purification of the saved|A lesser hell|A limbo|1|CCC 1030–1031.', 'What is the best way to help the dead, according to the Church?|Flowers|The offering of the Mass|Silence|A memorial plaque|1|CCC 1371.', 'Where does the Church ask that cremated remains be kept?|At home|In a sacred place such as a cemetery or columbarium|Scattered at sea|Anywhere|1|Ad resurgendum cum Christo, 2016.'],
   life='Choose one person who has died and have a Mass offered for them this month. Visit the grave, or light a candle at church.', saint='Monica: she asked only to be remembered at the altar of the Lord, wherever she died, and her son Augustine kept her request.',
   deeper=['room:aug-confessions'], mem='“Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord, and let perpetual light shine upon them.”', hook='Ask: “Whose name do you most wish you had said a prayer for last week?” Allow silence.', assess='Ask each person to define purgatory, name three ways to help the dead, and describe the three stages of a Catholic funeral.',
   tryit=['Write the names of four people to pray for.', 'Check the parish bulletin for how to request a Mass intention.', 'Find the date of All Souls’ Day and plan to attend.'],
   saintStory='Monica was born in 331 in Thagaste, North Africa, and was married as a young woman to Patricius, a pagan official with a bad temper. She had three children. She prayed for her husband’s conversion for years; he was baptized a year before his death in 371. Her eldest son, Augustine, was brilliant and restless, and drifted from the faith, living with a woman and joining the Manichaeans.\nFor seventeen years Monica prayed and wept for him. She followed him to Carthage, then to Rome and Milan. A bishop told her that “the son of so many tears cannot perish.” In Milan she met Bishop Ambrose, whose preaching helped Augustine. In 387 at the Easter Vigil, Augustine was baptized. That autumn, mother and son stopped at Ostia, the port of Rome, on their way home. In a famous conversation by a window, they spoke of the life of the saints in heaven, and sensed it for a moment.\nA few days later Monica fell ill and died at about fifty-six. Augustine tells us that she asked only that they remember her at the altar of the Lord. She had once wanted to be buried next to her husband, but now said nothing mattered where. Augustine wrote of his grief and of his prayers for her in the Confessions. Her memorial is August 27, the day before his.',
   script=dict(open='Place a single lit candle on the table and let the room be quiet for a moment. Then ask, “What is the first thing that comes to mind when you hear the word ‘funeral’?” Write responses without comment. Say, “Tonight we look at what the Church does and why.”',
     teach=['Say: “Purgatory is not a place in a Dante sense; it is a state of purification (CCC 1030–1031). The Church teaches that all who die in God’s grace and friendship, but imperfectly purified, are assured of their salvation, but still undergo a purification to achieve the holiness necessary to enter heaven. Biblical roots: 2 Maccabees 12:38–46, where Judas Maccabeus has sacrifices offered for his fallen soldiers; 1 Corinthians 3:15, where a person is saved ‘but only as through fire’; Matthew 12:32. Early Christians inscribed prayers for the dead in the catacombs. The Council of Trent confirmed the doctrine. Note: the Church has not defined how long, or whether it is a place, only that it is real.”', 'Say: “What can we do for the dead? The Church recommends: Mass, above all (CCC 1371); prayers; almsgiving; penance; indulgences, which are the remission before God of the temporal punishment due to sins already forgiven, gained under conditions and applicable to the dead (CCC 1471–1479). Our prayers join the communion of saints (CCC 954–959); this is not magic but an act of love across the barrier of death. The month of November is dedicated to the holy souls, and November 2 is All Souls’ Day, on which each priest may say three Masses.”', 'Say: “The Catholic funeral (CCC 1680–1690): the vigil, usually a wake with prayers and the rosary; the funeral Mass, with the white or purple vestments, the paschal candle, the pall, and the final commendation; and the rite of committal at the grave. The funeral Mass is for the deceased and for the comfort of the living; it is not a canonization, and eulogies are kept for before or after. Burial is preferred; cremation is permitted but ashes must be kept in a sacred place and not scattered (Ad resurgendum cum Christo, 2016). The Church calls burying the dead a work of mercy. Practical step tonight: write down your wishes and tell your family.”'],
     ask=['What is purgatory? (The final purification of those who die in grace.)', 'What is the best help we can give the dead? (The Mass.)', 'What are the three stages of a Catholic funeral? (Vigil, funeral liturgy, committal.)'],
     practice=['Read 2 Maccabees 12:38–46 together and note what Judas does and why.', 'Each person writes the names of four people to pray for and shares one.', 'Draft a short note of your wishes for a funeral and discuss who you will give it to.'],
     close='Pray together: “Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord, and let perpetual light shine upon them. May they rest in peace. Amen.” Invite each person to have a Mass offered this month.')),
 ]),
]
